	<h2>09. Getting Forward</h2>
	<div class="content-content">






<figure class="wp-block-image alignwide size-large"><img fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" width="1024" height="512" src="https://www.americanyawp.com/textbook/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/YAWP_Figure_9.1-1024x512.jpg" alt="This painting shows a busy port station in New York City in 1827. The left side of the painting depicts a row of merchant ships docked in a harbor. People line the foreground of the painting from left to right, many engaged in conversation with others, some are standing near horse draw carriages, some are sitting on barrels of hay, some are sitting on the street corners, some lean and sit on wooden barrels, some are walking into merchant shops, and one family standing to the center left of the scene points to the docked ships." class="wp-image-1993" srcset="https://www.americanyawp.com/textbook/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/YAWP_Figure_9.1-1024x512.jpg 1024w, https://www.americanyawp.com/textbook/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/YAWP_Figure_9.1-300x150.jpg 300w, https://www.americanyawp.com/textbook/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/YAWP_Figure_9.1-768x384.jpg 768w, https://www.americanyawp.com/textbook/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/YAWP_Figure_9.1.jpg 1133w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /><figcaption class="wp-element-caption">Figure 9.1. William James Bennett, View of South Street, from Maiden Lane, New York City, c. 1827, Metropolitan Museum of Art.</figcaption></figure>







